      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.28896_p14_b12",
      "text": "LoRA and PEFT: Hu et al. [2022] introduced LoRA; subsequent work explored variants\n[Dettmers et al., 2024, Zhao et al., 2024]. Hu et al. [2023] studied behavioural effects of fine-\ntuning, and Biderman et al. [2024] studied forgetting dynamics. None have studied fine-tuning\neffects at the feature level.\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        563.67,
        525.13,
        615.31
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        2254,
        2100,
        2461
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.02,
      "font_name": "CMBX10",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 304,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.28896_p14_b13",
      "text": "Mechanistic interpretability: SAEs have emerged as a key tool for decomposing superposed\nrepresentations [Bricken et al., 2023, Templeton et al., 2024]. Cunningham et al. [2023] trained\nSAEs on GPT-2; Gemma Scope [2024] released comprehensive SAE dictionaries for Gemma-2.\nAll prior work focuses on base models.\n",
      "bbox": [
        71.6,
        623.47,
        525.4,
        674.96
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        286,
        2493,
        2101,
        2699
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.83,
      "font_name": "CMBX10",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 311,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.28896_p14_b14",
      "text": "Fine-tuning and safety: Qi et al. [2023] and Yang et al. [2023] demonstrated that safety\nfine-tuning is fragile to subsequent fine-tuning. Evans et al. [2025] showed that narrow task fine-\ntuning on insecure code causes broad emergent misalignment. Our work provides a mechanistic\naccount at the feature level of why fine-tuning may escape base model safety constraints.\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        682.97,
        525.17,
        734.62
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        2731,
        2100,
        2938
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.02,
      "font_name": "CMBX10",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 370,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.28896_p14_b15",
      "text": "Representation similarity: CKA [Kornblith et al., 2019] and SVCCA [Raghu et al., 2017]\nhave been used to compare model representations across architectures and training runs; we\n",
